               <msContents>
               	<summary>Enclosed within some other textual material, the <title>Karaṇḍamudrādhāraṇī</title>, personalized to mention king Śubhārakadeva and his queen.</summary>
               </msContents>
               <physDesc>
                  <handDesc>
                  	<p>According to Tripathy, <q>Eastern variety of Devanagari characters of circa tenth-eleventh century.</q> The script makes no distinction between <foreign>b</foreign> and <foreign>v</foreign>, only the latter being used; the distinction between characters <foreign>dh/v</foreign> is <foreign>r/n</foreign> often ambiguous — or rather, it seems that the engraver was copying from an exemplar that he found ambiguous, and has made wrong choices more than once. Expected <foreign>anusvāra</foreign>s and <foreign>ā-mātrā</foreign>s are often not found or at least undiscernible in the available photographs.</p>
                  </handDesc>
               </physDesc>
            </msDesc>
